// Title: A Tiny TCP/IP Using Non-deterministic Parsing (body figure: the service-port grammar)
// Author: Ian Piumarta ("Principal Researcher" on the page)
// Source: VPRI TR-2007-008, STEPS 2007 first-year progress report (Viewpoints Research Institute, 2008), report body section "A Tiny TCP/IP Using Non-deterministic Parsing", p. 17 (PDF p. 17). A body figure, not Appendix E.
// Listing: the anonymous IS grammar object for the svc/syn/req/ack rules, as printed. It is the same grammar as the last block of Appendix E, with different line breaks.
// Explanatory prose omitted (the paragraphs before and after the listing).
//
// Transcribed from the page images; text-layer differences noted below.

['{ svc     = &->(svc? [self peek])
    syn     = &->(syn? [self peek]) .   ->(out ack-syn    -1 (+ sequenceNumber 1) (+ TCP_ACK TCP_SYN) 0)
    req     = &->(req? [self peek]) .   ->(out ack-psh-fin 0 (+ sequenceNumber datalen (fin-len tcp))
                                                             (+ TCP_ACK TCP_PSH TCP_FIN)
                                                             (up destinationPort dev ip tcp
                                                                 (tcp-payload tcp) datalen))
    ack     = &->(ack? [self peek]) .   ->(out ack           acknowledgementNumber
                                                             (+ sequenceNumber datalen (fin-len tcp))
                                                             TCP_ACK 0)
            ;
    ( svc (syn | req | ack | .) | .     ->(out ack-rst       acknowledgementNumber
                                                             (+ sequenceNumber 1)
                                                             (+ TCP_ACK TCP_RST) 0)
    ) *
  } < [NetworkPseudoInterface tunnel: '"/dev/tun0" from: '"10.0.0.1" to: '"10.0.0.2"]]

// ---- Transcription notes ----
// Checked against the 250 dpi page image: compared at about 850 px width only.
// Text-layer differences: none found in the comparison.
// Glyphs marked [?]: none. Spacing is reproduced from glyph positions, so runs of spaces are as printed.
